38:["$","section","When a card usually wins",{"className":"space-y-3","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-display text-xl font-bold tracking-tight","children":"When a card usually wins"}],[["$","p","You can clear the statement in full ever",{"className":"text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed","children":"You can clear the statement in full every month, you want reward/cashback economics you actually use, or you need a short float until salary credit. Interest-free periods only help if you do not carry a balance across statements."}],["$","p","For travel bookings and online spends wh",{"className":"text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed","children":"For travel bookings and online spends where rewards outweigh fees — and you still pay in full — a card can beat taking a loan you do not need. If you already revolve, stop adding spend before you “optimise rewards”."}]],null,null,null]}]
39:["$","section","When a personal loan usually wins",{"className":"space-y-3","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-display text-xl font-bold tracking-tight","children":"When a personal loan usually wins"}],[["$","p","You already carry card balances at revol",{"className":"text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed","children":"You already carry card balances at revolving rates, you need a defined payoff date, or the amount is large enough that card EMI conversion still looks expensive after fees. Run both EMIs side by side on the calculator before you convert."}],["$","p","Personal loans also keep utilisation on ",{"className":"text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed","children":"Personal loans also keep utilisation on cards lower once you pay them down — which can help bureau metrics over time if you then keep card balances light. That only works if you do not re-max the cards after the loan credits."}]],null,null,null]}]
3a:["$","section","Hybrid trap to avoid",{"className":"space-y-3","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-display text-xl font-bold tracking-tight","children":"Hybrid trap to avoid"}],[["$","p","Taking a personal loan and then maxing c",{"className":"text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed","children":"Taking a personal loan and then maxing cards again doubles FOIR. Treat the loan as debt reduction, not a licence for new revolving spend. Autopay both products from the salary account and track one payoff date on a calendar."}]],null,null,null]}]
3b:["$","section","Practical next steps on ContentVerse",{"className":"space-y-3","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-display text-xl font-bold tracking-tight","children":"Practical next steps on ContentVerse"}],[["$","p","Use the EMI calculator twice with the sa",{"className":"text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed","children":"Use the EMI calculator twice with the same principal and realistic tenures. Read the CIBIL guide if enquiries or late marks worry you. For card behaviour specifically, read the minimum-due explainer before you decide “EMI conversion is fine”."}],["$","p","If the loan is for consolidating card du",{"className":"text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed","children":"If the loan is for consolidating card dues, freeze new card spend in the same week the loan disburses. Track payoff in a simple sheet: principal left, EMI date, and the date you expect zero balance."}]],null,null,null]}]
